    "case_id": "481",
    "case_summary": "A 17-year-old Thai woman, 33 weeks and 4 days into her first pregnancy with no significant medical history, presented to the emergency room with a one-day history of fever, cough, and sore throat. She was carrying twins with estimated body weights of 1900 g and 2000 g at the time of presentation. The patient had no vaccination history during pregnancy.\n\nInitial presentation (00:33):- Temperature: 39.2°C- Pulse: 129 beats per minute- Respiratory rate: 22 breaths per minute- Blood pressure: 130/81 mm HgLater examination (20:30):- Cervix dilated to 3 cm- Temperature: 39.0°C- Pulse: 136 beats per minute- Respiratory rate: 20 breaths per minute- Blood pressure: 144/81 mm HgProgressive deterioration (21:00-21:30):- Respiratory rate increased to 28-30 breaths per minute- Oxygen saturation: 86% on room air, 96% on 10L oxygen- Blood pressure elevated to 200/120 mm Hg- Fine crepitations on lung examination- Pink frothy sputum noted on endotracheal aspiration\n\nLaboratory Tests:- White blood cell count: 13.4 × 10⁹/L- Platelet count: 139 × 10⁹/L- Hemoglobin: 112 g/L- Hematocrit: 0.361- Hemoculture: No pathogens grownImaging Studies:- Chest Radiograph  Title: Chest X-ray  Description: Patchy infiltrates in the upper and middle lobes of the right lungFetal Monitoring:- Initial stress test showed normal fetal heartbeats- Later monitoring showed fetal tachycardia- Final monitoring showed decreased fetal heart sounds (80-90 beats per minute)Other Tests:- Real-time PCR of lung and heart tissue: Positive for influenza A (H3N2) virus, negative for influenza B virus and 16 other common respiratory viruses",
